# Every text file is stored with LF and checked out with whatever this machine
# uses, rather than leaving the decision to git's guess on each clone.
#
# The guess can fail, and it fails the way everything else in this repository
# fails: quietly, one layer away. Git calls a blob binary as soon as its CR
# count and its CRLF count disagree - and a binary blob is never normalized and
# never diffed by line. `docs/TODO-provera.md` picked up two stray CR bytes
# (`\r\r\n`, the fingerprint of one conversion applied twice), and from then on
# every edit to it showed all 1100 lines as changed, with nothing anywhere to
# say why. This line decides it instead of the heuristic.
* text=auto

# Shell scripts must keep LF even when checked out on Windows: these are copied
# straight from the working copy to a Linux host, and CRLF makes bash fail with
# "$'\r': command not found".
*.sh text eol=lf

# A prompt whose bytes are the contract keeps LF too.
# `chess_backend/services/prompts/tutorial_words.txt` is read at runtime and
# must produce, byte for byte, the prompt `tools/game_annotate/skeleton.py`
# wrote into the fixtures under `chess_app/test/fixtures/game_tutorial/`;
# `chess_backend/test/tutorial_words.test.js` compares the two. Under
# `text=auto` alone, a clone on Windows checks the template out as CRLF and
# those ten tests go red on a machine that changed nothing - and the prompt
# the server actually sends stops being the prompt that was measured. Found
# on 18.9.2026 in a fresh agent worktree, which is a fresh checkout and so
# had the CRLF copy while this one had LF.
chess_backend/services/prompts/*.txt text eol=lf
